#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e228b3-ea11-58a0-b53c-2fc041457b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341235} {:answer/id #uuid "a99a637b-f75a-5bd3-8aca-2dbc6513b0df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341236} {:answer/id #uuid "a11f718c-8495-5429-afd8-5751fd26eb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341237} {:answer/id #uuid "e795cc2e-1c35-55b2-a55e-6b487a6505fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341238} {:answer/id #uuid "4e291f10-eac8-5118-a744-1c754ab2b3a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}x \\\\ \\sqrt{3}y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03e228b3-ea11-58a0-b53c-2fc041457b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341235} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f4786a8-145a-572c-aeef-afd1f4d4920f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d45dd63c-10bf-5009-8d17-f860f39bde5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-126921" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{3},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "366bae8c-726f-542e-bf94-fe7f1b1eabf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341242} {:answer/id #uuid "cb84497f-cacd-537c-a5fb-8b0f3c3f5dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341243} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ee63ac-e434-5087-992a-d6a4e80cb4d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341244} {:answer/id #uuid "a22fc778-d5e3-5d4f-bd21-cdf2cc4c08b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341245} {:answer/id #uuid "c62a56bf-ea4f-5794-9315-e3807b48ec77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a22fc778-d5e3-5d4f-bd21-cdf2cc4c08b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341245} :answer-field/id #uuid "b10b49c9-e8d8-53bb-a2c7-42b5e6aa0fca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "758f5712-a105-5a05-882e-5364cee33958" :question/math-academy-id "q-126923" :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5703010e-d3c3-5edc-86ab-f0dfe3ade8f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341249} {:answer/id #uuid "4ade578b-9250-5914-a0bc-e00812b2a5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341250} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1ca206-6b34-53fd-ab7d-39595e92769a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341251} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9d3b5e-b868-5c34-ad6d-4a0d190299b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341252} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5220cb-36ae-5f2a-98fa-4021022baf19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb9d3b5e-b868-5c34-ad6d-4a0d190299b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341252} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb1b74e4-e59e-5d4b-ac45-e309cdcd3eab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72ae09ab-e5f0-51d8-81a2-5a0717c12ea9" :question/math-academy-id "q-126929" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}15\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively 